00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:34.860
the stock quoted at $41.90 bid and $42.40-ask, what happened? Your market order swept through

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:41.400
several thin liquidity layers and you paid an effective spread of roughly $0.50 per share, about 1.2%

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:49.380
on a $42 stock. On a $10,000 position that's $60 to $100 of immediate permanent slippage you will

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:55.340
never recover. On days when geopolitical optimism drives broad index gains, but individual names see

00:06:55.340 --> 00:07:02.560
only modest volume spikes, average bid-ask spreads on TSX, listed technology and financial stocks can

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:08.800
easily widen from their normal five to eight cent range all the way up to 25 to 40 cents in the first

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:15.680
30 to 45 minutes after the open. That friction compounds fast. The pro-tip most retail investor still

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.720
miss is to always check the level two order book depth and the 20-day average daily volume before

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:26.380
you hit the buy button on any catalyst-driven name. Professionals rarely chase. Instead they set

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:31.320
limit orders 10 to 15 cents inside the current ask and let natural sellers come to them.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.720
The single biggest mistake is assuming that the nice tight quote you see on your brokerage app reflects

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:44.080
true executable liquidity. It often doesn't, especially in the Canadian mid cap universe. My rule of

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:50.940
thumb verify that 20-day average daily volume exceeds 500,000 shares and only place market orders when

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:58.020
the bid-ask spread is narrower than 0.4% of the share price. Otherwise, use limit orders and patience.

